 - Product owner, 2026-10-14 (links): the floor is that a link may not cost what ⌃⌥Z cannot bring back. Above it, `arrange`, its styled forms on a whole display, `cycle-layout` and `layout` stay refused where they would cost a saved view or a name, although undo restores them. What takes several links and has a way back each time is not guarded. `arbor://save-view/N` is refused where N is taken.
 - Product owner, 2026-10-14 (`arborctl … > /dev/full`): by design: the exit code says whether Arbor did it, not whether the shell could print it.
 - Product owner, 2026-10-14 (how a claim is run, third lesson): ask for the RIGHT place, not only a whole window; let time pass between two reports a simulator delivers at once; press the keys a person presses, not the commands behind them; after every release add a step that must move something. The replays are under `scripts/replays` on the branch `tools/decision-replays`.
+- F59: the arrow keys move an imaginary pointer through the targets a drag has, from a fixed point in each; they do not go by the previews' middles, as the spec first says, because that leaves the edges of the view unreachable.
+- F59: two targets are one choice only if the whole arrangement would look the same afterwards. How a tile is arranged inside does not count: to choose a place in there, look closer first.
+- F59: `carry` with a window in hand puts it down (the key that picks up also lets go); `carry <another window>` puts the first back and picks that one up; ⌃⌥⌘[ and ⌃⌥⌘] pick the focused window up if none is in hand.
+- F59: the carried window keeps the keyboard, in view or not. The keyboard going to any other window cancels, as do a mouse drag or resize, a change of settings, displays or held shortcuts, the window closing or being minimized, and 30 seconds without a key. The plain keys are keys people type with.
+- F59: a mouse drag or resize ends a carry but leaves the view as it is (⌃⌥[ goes back): picking up may have widened the view, and putting it back could take the window the mouse has just taken out from under the pointer. Found by the chaos lab.
+- F59: when the view changes and what was chosen is not in it any more, the carry arrives at the nearest edge of the new view, which puts the window into the space being looked at. Beside the one window in view can mean its row, beyond the view.
+- F59: dropped on the Desktop, a docked window floats in the middle of it at the size it floated at. The Desktop's four edges are places too, which a pointer cannot choose, also while the Desktop is only a tile.
+- F59: the preview shows during a carry even with `previewDrags` off: a carry has nothing else to show where it is.
+- F59: a pinned window is not carried (it never docks by a drag either; the reply says to unpin it). `arbor://carry` is refused: a web page must not take the arrow keys, ↩, Space and ⎋.
