+### Put a window exactly there, without the mouse
+
+**⌃⌥⌘Space** picks up the window you are typing in. A preview shows where it would land, starting where it is. The **arrow keys** walk the preview through every place a drag could choose: toward a window they offer its near edge, then its far edge, then
+what lies beyond, and ↑ and ↓ its top and bottom. **↩** or Space puts the window down; **⎋** puts everything back. So "Slack under Terminal" is ⌃⌥⌘Space → ↓ ↩.
+The view shortcuts still work while you carry: ⌃⌥2, then ↩, takes the window into the space you saved as view 2. ⌃⌥⌘] carries it to the next display. The Desktop is a place like any other: put a docked window down there and it floats.
+Nothing moves until you put the window down, and if you do anything else (another shortcut, a click in another window, nothing at all for half a minute) it is as if you had pressed ⎋.
